\title{Hidden markov model}
\taxon{Definition}
\tag{public}

\p{A hidden markov model is a statistical model, where the \em{hidden states} #{h \in \mathcal{H}} form a [[0040]], such that #{\mathbb{P}(h_{t+1} \mid h_{1}, \ldots, h_{t}) = \mathbb{P}(h_{t + 1} \mid h_{t})}}

\p{Unlike a Markov chain, in addition to the hidden states, a hidden markov models contains observations #{x \in \mathcal{X}} with associated \em{emission probabilities} #{\mathbb{P}(x_t \mid h_t)}.}

\figure{
\<html:img>[width]{80\%}[src]{\route-asset{assets/img/hmm.png}}{}
\figcaption{Graphical model of a hidden markov model.}
}

\p{From the graphical model, we can see that for any #{i < j}, #{(x_{0}, \ldots, x_i) \perp (x_{j}, \ldots, x_{N}) \mid z_{i}}. Or in english, \em{observations are independent conditioned on the hidden state}. }
